-- title:
--   Residual Hecke eigensystem from residual modularity
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime, $W$ a Weierstrass curve over $\mathbb{Z}$, and $\mathcal{O}$ a commutative local ring in which the image of $p$ lies in the maximal ideal. Let $S$ be a finite set of natural numbers, all of whose members are prime, containing $p$, containing every prime $q$ with $q \mid W.\Delta$, and, for a level $N \neq 0$, containing every prime dividing $N$. Assume $W$ is residually modular of level $N$ at $p$: there are a weight-$2$ cusp form $f$ on $\Gamma_0(N)$ and a maximal ideal $\mathfrak{m}$ of the integral closure of $\mathbb{Z}$ in $\mathbb{C}$ with $p \in \mathfrak{m}$ such that $f$ is normalised ($q$-coefficient $1$ at $1$, multiplicative at coprime indices, with the usual weight-$2$ recursions at prime powers) and, for every prime $\ell$ with $\ell \nmid W.\Delta$, $\ell \nmid N$ and $\ell \neq p$, the $\ell$-th $q$-coefficient of $f$ is an algebraic integer congruent to $a_\ell(W) = \#\mathbb{F}_\ell + 1 - \#W_{/\mathbb{F}_\ell}$ modulo $\mathfrak{m}$. Assume also that the space of weight-$2$ cusp forms on $\Gamma_0(N)$ is spanned over $\mathbb{C}$ by the $\mathbb{Z}$-lattice of forms with integral $q$-coefficients. Then there is a ring homomorphism $\theta$ from [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra N 2 ↑S`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18), the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra of $\mathbb{C}$-endomorphisms of that space generated by the operators $T_\ell$ for primes $\ell \nmid N$, $\ell \notin S$ together with the $U_q$ for primes $q \mid N$, $q \notin S$, to the residue field of $\mathcal{O}$, such that for every prime $\ell$ with $\ell \nmid N$ and $\ell \notin S$ one has $\theta(T_\ell) =$ the residue of the image of $a_\ell(W)$ in $\mathcal{O}$.
--
--   This is the construction of the residual eigensystem, equivalently of the maximal ideal of the anaemic Hecke algebra of weight $2$ and level $N$ attached to the mod-$p$ representation of $W$, at which the Hecke algebra is localised in the Taylor–Wiles argument. It is the form in which residual modularity enters the Hecke side: the statement is cited by the constructions of patching data and by the local Hecke-algebra results used in the modularity lifting step.
